  "contracts": {
    "def-td-finite-microstate-model": {
      "citations": [],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. Boundary empty: The statement explicitly requires a nonempty state set, nonzero sector, nonempty shell, or positive trace; no empty-model conclusion is claimed.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. The physical energy of $x\\in\\Omega$ is $E(x)=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$, with its zero and any retained internal offsets fixed by the declared material model. Energies are evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame specified by [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], and $U$ is the corresponding macroscopic internal-energy coordinate of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Equal energy values at distinct elements of $\\Omega$ count as distinct microstates.\n\nWhen two such models are declared independent, their joint microstate set is the Cartesian product $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$, and additivity of the microscopic energy is the explicit condition $\\epsilon_{AB}(x,y)=\\epsilon_A(x)+\\epsilon_B(y)$. Independence and this energy equation are model data; they do not follow merely from the two systems being separated. The definition itself assigns no weights, temperature, probability law, or dynamics.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "9ec3e3e64df348a30e77000cdbf026283f92ef5979ac7c658e4571a0ae871472",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. Case zero: Zero-energy levels are admitted where the model allows them and contribute weight e^0=1; positive-temperature assumptions exclude a zero-temperature limit (Statement).",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Definition",
            "source_excerpt": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. The physical energy of $x\\in\\Omega$ is $E(x)=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$, with its zero and any retained internal offsets fixed by the declared material model. Energies are evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame specified by [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], and $U$ is the corresponding macroscopic internal-energy coordinate of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Equal energy values at distinct elements of $\\Omega$ count as distinct microstates.\n\nWhen two such models are declared independent, their joint microstate set is the Cartesian product $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$, and additivity of the microscopic energy is the explicit condition $\\epsilon_{AB}(x,y)=\\epsilon_A(x)+\\epsilon_B(y)$. Independence and this energy equation are model data; they do not follow merely from the two systems being separated. The definition itself assigns no weights, temperature, probability law, or dynamics.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "9ec3e3e64df348a30e77000cdbf026283f92ef5979ac7c658e4571a0ae871472",
