    "pthm-td-differentiable-pressure-concentration": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the finite-alphabet, bounded, translation-invariant finite-range lattice model of [[def-tdmath-bounded-finite-range-lattice]], with physical interaction energies in joules, inverse temperature $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}>0$, and partition sum $Z_L(\\beta)$. Suppose each site represents a fixed cell of volume $v_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{m^3}$. Define the reduced log-partition density $p_L=L^{-d}\\log Z_L$ and the lattice pressure convention $P_L=(k_{\\mathrm B}T/v_0)p_L$ in pascals. Then $p_L$ and $P_L$ have finite limits as $L\\to\\infty$. If a second boundary-energy model on the same configuration sets differs pointwise by at most $C_bL^{d-1}$ joules for fixed $C_b$, it has the same limit.\n\nThe pressure convention in this statement is assigned to this lattice model. Equating it with a derivative-based macroscopic pressure or a measured mechanical pressure requires a separate model identification.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-limiting-derivative-and-concentration",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the lattice models of [[thm-tdmath-finite-range-lattice-pressure-limit]], write $p_L(\\gamma)=L^{-d}\\log Z_L(\\gamma)$ and let $p(\\gamma)=\\lim_Lp_L(\\gamma)$ on an open interval $J\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$. At finite $L$, use the canonical law $\\mathbb P_{\\gamma,L}(s):=e^{-\\gamma H_L(s)}/Z_L(\\gamma)$ and its finite expectation $\\mathbb E_{\\gamma,L}$. If $p$ is differentiable at $\\beta\\in J$, then $\\mathbb E_{\\beta,L}[H_L]/L^d\\to e:=-p'(\\beta)$. For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there are $c>0$ and $L_0$ such that for $L\\ge L_0$, $\\mathbb P_{\\beta,L}(|H_L/L^d-e|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le2e^{-cL^d}$. No energy-density limit is asserted at a nondifferentiability point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m³); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The finite-volume energy law and $p_L$ in [F1]–[F2] match the model and probability hypotheses of [L1]. The condition $\\beta>0$ and differentiability at $\\beta$ are exactly those in [A1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Applying [L1] yields $\\mathbb E_{\\beta,L}[H_L]/L^d\\to -p'(\\beta)=e$ and, for each fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, a constant $c>0$ and threshold $L_0$ with the stated two-tail exponential estimate. The result is a model probability statement and is silent at pressure nondifferentiability.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For the lattice model and canonical law of [[pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure]], let $p_L(\\gamma)=L^{-d}\\log Z_L(\\gamma)$ converge pointwise on an open interval $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ to $p(\\gamma)$. Boundary empty: The statement explicitly requires a nonempty state set, nonzero sector, nonempty shell, or positive trace; no empty-model conclusion is claimed.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "For the lattice model and canonical law of [[pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure]], let $p_L(\\gamma)=L^{-d}\\log Z_L(\\gamma)$ converge pointwise on an open interval $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ to $p(\\gamma)$. If $p$ is differentiable at $\\beta\\in J_\\beta$, set $e=-p'(\\beta)$ in joules per site. Then the canonical mean energy density satisfies $\\mathbb E_{\\beta,L}[H_L]/L^d\\to e$, and for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there exist $c>0$ and $L_0$ such that\n$$\\mathbb P_{\\beta,L}\\!\\left(\\left|H_L/L^d-e\\right|\\ge\\varepsilon\\right)\\le2e^{-cL^d}\\qquad(L\\ge L_0).$$\nThe probability is the model's finite-volume canonical probability, not an observed frequency. No conclusion is asserted when $p$ is nondifferentiable.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "ebb5924692a683906b3d8819dde38645290bda612a9914b7424abd1f35a729c0",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: For the lattice model and canonical law of [[pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure]], let $p_L(\\gamma)=L^{-d}\\log Z_L(\\gamma)$ converge pointwise on an open interval $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ to $p(\\gamma)$. Case zero: Zero-energy levels are admitted where the model allows them and contribute weight e^0=1; positive-temperature assumptions exclude a zero-temperature limit (Statement).",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "For the lattice model and canonical law of [[pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure]], let $p_L(\\gamma)=L^{-d}\\log Z_L(\\gamma)$ converge pointwise on an open interval $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ to $p(\\gamma)$. If $p$ is differentiable at $\\beta\\in J_\\beta$, set $e=-p'(\\beta)$ in joules per site. Then the canonical mean energy density satisfies $\\mathbb E_{\\beta,L}[H_L]/L^d\\to e$, and for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there exist $c>0$ and $L_0$ such that\n$$\\mathbb P_{\\beta,L}\\!\\left(\\left|H_L/L^d-e\\right|\\ge\\varepsilon\\right)\\le2e^{-cL^d}\\qquad(L\\ge L_0).$$\nThe probability is the model's finite-volume canonical probability, not an observed frequency. No conclusion is asserted when $p$ is nondifferentiable.",
