    "pthm-td-finite-bath-canonical-error": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. The physical energy of $x\\in\\Omega$ is $E(x)=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$, with its zero and any retained internal offsets fixed by the declared material model. Energies are evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame specified by [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], and $U$ is the corresponding macroscopic internal-energy coordinate of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Equal energy values at distinct elements of $\\Omega$ count as distinct microstates.\n\nWhen two such models are declared independent, their joint microstate set is the Cartesian product $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$, and additivity of the microscopic energy is the explicit condition $\\epsilon_{AB}(x,y)=\\epsilon_A(x)+\\epsilon_B(y)$. Independence and this energy equation are model data; they do not follow merely from the two systems being separated. The definition itself assigns no weights, temperature, probability law, or dynamics.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "post-td-microcanonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "For a finite microstate model and a declared energy value $E_0$ in joules and resolution $\\delta\\ge0$ in joules, define the admitted window\n$$\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}:=\\{x\\in\\Omega:|E(x)-E_0|\\le\\delta\\}.$$\nWhen this set is nonempty, adopt the uniform preparation $P(x)=|\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}|^{-1}$ on it and zero outside. Its dimensionless Boltzmann entropy is $\\log|\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}|$, and its physical entropy convention is $k_{\\mathrm B}\\log|\\Omega_{E_0,\\delta}|$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. A zero-width window is allowed only when it contains at least one microstate.\n\nFor a continuous classical model, when the hypotheses and normalization in [[def-td-regular-hamiltonian-shell-preparation]] hold, the microcanonical preparation is the normalized shell measure $\\nu_{\\hat E}$ defined there. These finite-window and regular-shell rules are adopted model preparations. They do not assert Hamiltonian ergodicity, equality with long-time averages, canonical sampling, or equilibration.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-finite-bath-error-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a finite nonempty subsystem state set, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfy $|E_i|\\le L$ for $L\\ge0$, and let the dimensionless bath entropy $s_B$ be $C^2$ on an open interval $U$ containing $[u_0-L,u_0+L]$, with $|s_B''(u)|\\le M$ on that closed interval for $M\\ge0$. Use one energy unit for $E_i,u_0,L$, so $\\beta=s_B'(u_0)$ has inverse-energy units and $M$ has inverse-energy-squared units; $\\delta=ML^2/2$ is dimensionless. Put $Z:=\\sum_{j\\in I}e^{-\\beta E_j}$ and $q_i:=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$. Suppose the joint shell weights induce the normalized subsystem law $p_i:=e^{s_B(u_0-E_i)}/\\sum_{j\\in I}e^{s_B(u_0-E_j)}$. Then $|s_B(u_0-E_i)-s_B(u_0)+\\beta E_i|\\le\\delta$ for every $i$, and $\\sum_i|p_i-q_i|\\le e^{2\\delta}-1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "With $|E_x|\\le L$, every bath argument $u_0-E_x$ lies in the stated interval. The curvature bound and $\\beta=s'_B(u_0)$ therefore match the hypotheses of [L1], including the dimensionless remainder scale $\\delta=ML^2/2$. The shell weights in [F3] are exactly the weights required by [L1], and the finite canonical comparison is [F2].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Applying [L1] to these matched inputs gives $|s_B(u_0-E_x)-s_B(u_0)+\\beta E_x|\\le\\delta$ for each state and $\\sum_x|P_B(x)-P_C(x)|\\le e^{2\\delta}-1$. The claim is conditional on the actual shell-weight rule and the draft mathematical supplier, not on bath size alone.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement finite Omega is explicitly nonempty. The adopted weak-coupling shell weights and canonical exponentials normalize over that same set; the theorem supplies no probability vector on an empty subsystem.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let the subsystem have a finite nonempty state set $\\Omega$ and internal energies $E_x$ in joules with $|E_x|\\le L$. A bath has a dimensionless shell entropy $s_B(u)$, defined on an open interval containing $[u_0-L,u_0+L]$ in joules, with $s_B\\in C^2$ and $|s_B''(u)|\\le M$ there; the model adopts $\\beta=s'_B(u_0)>0$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$ and $T=(k_{\\mathrm B}\\beta)^{-1}$. Assume weak additive coupling and microcanonical shell sampling induce subsystem weights $P_B(x)\\propto e^{s_B(u_0-E_x)}$. Let $P_C(x)=e^{-\\beta E_x}/Z$ be the corresponding canonical law. With $\\delta=ML^2/2$ (dimensionless), the shell log-weight remainder obeys\n$$|s_B(u_0-E_x)-s_B(u_0)+\\beta E_x|\\le\\delta,$$\nand the normalized laws satisfy\n$$\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}|P_B(x)-P_C(x)|\\le e^{2\\delta}-1.$$\nThis is an $\\ell^1$ probability-vector bound; it does not assert a universal error or a thermodynamic limit.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "ba3f741efcd3f15cfab3e6d82461c0f47d41be11bd0f33ea9dc35bfceb9bb2c2",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
