    "pthm-td-finite-entropy-maximum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-td-finite-microstate-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. The physical energy of $x\\in\\Omega$ is $E(x)=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$, with its zero and any retained internal offsets fixed by the declared material model. Energies are evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame specified by [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], and $U$ is the corresponding macroscopic internal-energy coordinate of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Equal energy values at distinct elements of $\\Omega$ count as distinct microstates.\n\nWhen two such models are declared independent, their joint microstate set is the Cartesian product $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$, and additivity of the microscopic energy is the explicit condition $\\epsilon_{AB}(x,y)=\\epsilon_A(x)+\\epsilon_B(y)$. Independence and this energy equation are model data; they do not follow merely from the two systems being separated. The definition itself assigns no weights, temperature, probability law, or dynamics.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "pthm-td-finite-canonical-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ be a finite microstate model with energies $E_x=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$ from [[def-td-finite-microstate-model]]. Let $T>0$, $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$, and $P_\\beta(x)=e^{-\\beta E_x}/Z(\\beta)$ be the adopted preparation of [[post-td-canonical-sampling]]. With $\\bar E_\\beta=\\sum_xP_\\beta(x)E_x$, $\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)=\\sum_xP_\\beta(x)(E_x-\\bar E_\\beta)^2$, and $S_\\beta=-k_{\\mathrm B}\\sum_xP_\\beta(x)\\log P_\\beta(x)$, the finite partition sum is positive and\n$$-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\bar E_\\beta,\\qquad \\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E),\\qquad S_\\beta=k_{\\mathrm B}(\\log Z+\\beta\\bar E_\\beta).$$\nConsequently the statistical free-energy value $F_{\\mathrm{stat}}:=-k_{\\mathrm B}T\\log Z$ satisfies $F_{\\mathrm{stat}}=\\bar E_\\beta-TS_\\beta$. The derivatives hold the finite state set and its energy list fixed. No equivalence with a macroscopic entropy or Helmholtz potential is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-logarithm-derivative-and-integral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, $\\log$ is differentiable and\n$$\\log'(x)=\\frac1x,\\qquad \\log x=\\int_1^x\\frac{dt}{t}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For every $t>0$, [F4] gives $\\log t\\le t-1$, with equality only at $t=1$: for $t>1$, integrate $1/u<1$ on $(1,t)$; for $0<t<1$, integrate $1/u>1$ on $(t,1)$ and reverse the endpoints. For every $x$ with $r_x>0$, apply this to $t=q_x/r_x$ to obtain $r_x\\log(r_x/q_x)\\ge r_x-q_x$. Summing over the support of $r$ gives $D:=\\sum_{x:r_x>0}r_x\\log(r_x/q_x)\\ge1-\\sum_{x:r_x>0}q_x\\ge0$. Equality forces full support and $r_x=q_x$ for every state.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Using [F3] and the fixed-mean condition [A1], $D=\\sum_{x:r_x>0}r_x\\log r_x+\\beta\\bar E+\\log Z$. Therefore $S(r)=k_{\\mathrm B}(\\beta\\bar E+\\log Z-D)\\le k_{\\mathrm B}(\\beta\\bar E+\\log Z)=S(q)$ by the finite identity in [F3]. Equality holds only when $D=0$, which by step 1.1 is equivalent to $r=q$. This proves the unique maximum.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement the microstate model is explicitly finite and nonempty, and the competing r vectors sum to one. An empty state set cannot satisfy this normalization or attain the specified energy.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ be a finite nonempty microstate model, let $T>0$, and let $q_x=e^{-\\beta E_x}/Z$ be its canonical probabilities at $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$. Set $\\bar E=\\sum_xq_xE_x$. Among all probability vectors $r=(r_x)_{x\\in\\Omega}$ with $\\sum_xr_x=1$ and $\\sum_xr_xE_x=\\bar E$, the Shannon entropy $S(r):=-k_{\\mathrm B}\\sum_{x:r_x>0}r_x\\log r_x$ is uniquely maximized by $r=q$. This is a finite-model variational theorem; it does not establish that a physical sample is prepared canonically.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "7d54dd0ce2a297203d040355819687ce28393ffd63c6d5d821d5ca9600cc5120",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
