    "pthm-td-lattice-bulk-pressure": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-bounded-finite-range-lattice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix integers $d\\ge1$, $L\\ge1$, a finite alphabet $\\mathcal A$ of cardinality $q\\ge1$, and a finite, possibly empty, list of interaction shapes $S_a\\subset\\mathbb Z^d$, each nonempty and contained in $[-R,R]^d$ for some integer $R\\ge0$. $\\|\\Phi_a\\|_\\infty$ is finite because its domain is finite. For each shape let $\\Phi_a:\\mathcal A^{S_a}\\to\\mathbb R$ be a bounded interaction function. Set $J_0:=\\sum_a\\|\\Phi_a\\|_\\infty$. On $\\Lambda_L:=\\{1,\\ldots,L\\}^d$, define the free-boundary energy $H_L(s):=\\sum_a\\sum_{x:x+S_a\\subseteq\\Lambda_L}\\Phi_a((s_{x+u})_{u\\in S_a})$, for configurations $s\\in\\mathcal A^{\\Lambda_L}$. For $\\beta>0$, define the finite partition sum $Z_L(\\beta):=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal A^{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta H_L(s)}$ and normalized log-partition density $p_L(\\beta):=L^{-d}\\log Z_L(\\beta)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-finite-range-lattice-pressure-limit",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every fixed $\\beta>0$, the sequence $p_L(\\beta)$ of [[def-tdmath-bounded-finite-range-lattice]] has a finite limit as $L\\to\\infty$. If another finite-volume energy $\\widetilde H_L$ on the same configurations satisfies $\\sup_s|\\widetilde H_L(s)-H_L(s)|\\le C_bL^{d-1}$ for a constant $C_b$ independent of $L$, define $\\widetilde Z_L(\\beta):=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal A^{\\Lambda_L}}e^{-\\beta\\widetilde H_L(s)}$ and $\\widetilde p_L(\\beta):=L^{-d}\\log\\widetilde Z_L(\\beta)$. Then $\\widetilde p_L$ has the same limit. The result covers finite-alphabet, bounded, translation-invariant finite-range interactions on cubic boxes; it asserts no continuum-particle or long-range limit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m³); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The finite lattice data in [F1] and $\\beta>0$ match the model hypotheses of [F2]. Thus $p_L=L^{-d}\\log Z_L$ converges to a finite $p_\\infty$; the same supplier gives the identical limit for the stated boundary perturbation. By [F4], these finite partition sums give the adopted lattice canonical law.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since $v_0$ and $T$ are fixed along the volume sequence, multiplying by the constant $k_{\\mathrm B}T/v_0$ preserves convergence: $P_L=(k_{\\mathrm B}T/v_0)p_L\\to(k_{\\mathrm B}T/v_0)p_\\infty$. The factor has units of pressure by [F3]. This proves only the declared lattice pressure convention.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Use the finite-alphabet, bounded, translation-invariant finite-range lattice model of [[def-tdmath-bounded-finite-range-lattice]], with physical interaction energies in joules, inverse temperature $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}>0$, and partition sum $Z_L(\\beta)$. Boundary empty: The statement explicitly requires a nonempty state set, nonzero sector, nonempty shell, or positive trace; no empty-model conclusion is claimed.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Use the finite-alphabet, bounded, translation-invariant finite-range lattice model of [[def-tdmath-bounded-finite-range-lattice]], with physical interaction energies in joules, inverse temperature $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}>0$, and partition sum $Z_L(\\beta)$. Suppose each site represents a fixed cell of volume $v_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{m^3}$. Define the reduced log-partition density $p_L=L^{-d}\\log Z_L$ and the lattice pressure convention $P_L=(k_{\\mathrm B}T/v_0)p_L$ in pascals. Then $p_L$ and $P_L$ have finite limits as $L\\to\\infty$. If a second boundary-energy model on the same configuration sets differs pointwise by at most $C_bL^{d-1}$ joules for fixed $C_b$, it has the same limit.\n\nThe pressure convention in this statement is assigned to this lattice model. Equating it with a derivative-based macroscopic pressure or a measured mechanical pressure requires a separate model identification.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "c2dcf743a7dbc99d4ef4da2418f749b78f431d11193d7b85798424472fa6b810",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
