    "pthm-td-measurable-canonical-identities": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-tdmath-measure-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A measure space is a triple $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ where $\\Sigma$ contains $\\varnothing,X$ and is closed under complements and countable unions (a $\\sigma$-algebra) of subsets of $X$ and $\\mu:\\Sigma\\to[0,\\infty]$ is countably additive with $\\mu(\\varnothing)=0$. A measurable function $f:X\\to[-\\infty,\\infty]$ has measurable inverse images of Borel sets, where the Borel sets of the extended real line are the sigma-algebra generated by its open sets. A nonnegative simple function has the form $s=\\sum_{j=1}^m c_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ with finitely many disjoint $A_j\\in\\Sigma$ and finite $c_j\\ge0$. Define $c\\mu(A)=+\\infty$ if $c>0$ and $\\mu(A)=+\\infty$, define $0\\cdot\\infty:=0$, and define the finite sum of these extended nonnegative terms to be $+\\infty$ if any term is $+\\infty$ and otherwise the real finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]]. Then $\\int s\\,d\\mu:=\\sum_j c_j\\mu(A_j)$; using canonical level sets $\\{s=c\\}$ makes this value representation-independent. For measurable $f\\ge0$, define $\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\sup\\{\\int s\\,d\\mu: s\\text{ is simple and }0\\le s\\le f\\}$, using [[def-complete-ordered-field]] for a bounded set of finite values and setting the supremum to $+\\infty$ when the values are unbounded or one is infinite. A real measurable $f$ is integrable when $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$, and then $\\int f:=\\int f^+-\\int f^-$, where $f^+=\\max(f,0)$ and $f^-:=\\max(-f,0)$. Complex integrability and integration are defined by integrable real and imaginary parts.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-tdmath-measurable-canonical-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $E:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable, and let $J\\subset\\mathbb R$ be open. Suppose $Z(\\beta):=\\int e^{-\\beta E}\\,d\\mu$ satisfies $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ on $J$, and for every compact interval $K\\subset J$ there is $G_K\\in L^1(\\mu)$ with $(1+|E|^2)e^{-\\beta E}\\le G_K$ pointwise for every $\\beta\\in K$. Define $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z(\\beta)$, $\\langle Y\\rangle_\\beta:=\\int Yp_\\beta\\,d\\mu$ for integrable $Y$, and $\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E):=\\int(E-\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta)^2p_\\beta\\,d\\mu$. Then $Z\\in C^2(J)$, $Z'=-\\int E e^{-\\beta E}d\\mu$, $Z''=\\int E^2e^{-\\beta E}d\\mu$, and $-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta$, $\\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$. The reference-relative entropy $-\\int p_\\beta\\log p_\\beta\\,d\\mu$ is finite and equals $\\log Z+\\beta\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m³); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The hypotheses in the Statement match [L1], including positive finite $Z$ and the integrable envelope on each compact parameter interval. The density in [F2] is therefore normalized and has finite first and second energy moments.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Applying [L1] yields the $C^2$ partition integral, logarithmic mean and variance identities, and the finite relative entropy formula. Multiplying the dimensionless entropy by $k_{\\mathrm B}$ gives $S_\\beta=k_{\\mathrm B}(\\log Z+\\beta\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta)$; since $\\beta k_{\\mathrm B}=1/T$, rearranging gives $F_{\\mathrm{stat}}=\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta-TS_\\beta=-k_{\\mathrm B}T\\log Z$. This conclusion is conditional on the draft mathematical supplier.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space with dimensionless reference measure, let $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ be measurable, and let $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ be open. Boundary empty: The statement explicitly requires a nonempty state set, nonzero sector, nonempty shell, or positive trace; no empty-model conclusion is claimed.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Let $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu)$ be a measure space with dimensionless reference measure, let $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ be measurable, and let $J_\\beta\\subset(0,\\infty)$ be open. Suppose $Z(\\beta)=\\int_Xe^{-\\beta E}\\,d\\mu$ is positive and finite on $J_\\beta$, and for each compact $K\\subset J_\\beta$ there is an integrable $G_K\\ge0$ with $(1+|E|^2)e^{-\\beta E}\\le G_K$ for every $\\beta\\in K$. Under the canonical sampling assumption [[post-td-canonical-sampling]], define $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$, $\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta=\\int E p_\\beta\\,d\\mu$, and $\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)=\\int(E-\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta)^2p_\\beta\\,d\\mu$. Then $Z\\in C^2$, $-\\partial_\\beta\\log Z=\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta$, $\\partial_\\beta^2\\log Z=\\operatorname{Var}_\\beta(E)$, and the relative Gibbs entropy is finite with $S_\\beta=-k_{\\mathrm B}\\int p_\\beta\\log p_\\beta\\,d\\mu=k_{\\mathrm B}(\\log Z+\\beta\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta)$. Hence $F_{\\mathrm{stat}}=-k_{\\mathrm B}T\\log Z=\\langle E\\rangle_\\beta-TS_\\beta$ for $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "786a6bd0aad63ad3755203b83e157039d52cd36daee938ece83c7ac153dfc1e7",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
