    "pthm-td-finite-mode-occupation-and-trace-formulas": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "post-nrqm-many-body-canonical-equilibrium-ensemble",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "**(a) Adopted readings and framework data.** Adopt the state and composition\npostulate [[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]] (P1), the observable\nand probability postulate\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]] (P2), the\nautonomous-dynamics postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]]\n(P4) and the preparation and apparatus postulate\n[[post-nrqm-framework-preparation-and-apparatus]] (P7), together with the\nBoltzmann-constant and temperature conventions of\n[[def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy]]. Declare one of the\nfollowing model readings of the same **adopted Gibbs assignment**:\n\n(i) a fixed-$N$ model of\n[[def-nrqm-many-body-framework-declared-fixed-n-model]], on its sector space,\nwith Gibbs generator $G:=H_N^\\sigma$; or\n\n(ii) a separately declared number-variable mode model, such as a one-mode\nbosonic Fock space, a fermionic occupation space, or a declared countable\nindependent-mode space, with its declared assigned observables and energy\noperator $H$. The model declares a self-adjoint Gibbs generator $G$ on that\nspace: either $G=H$, or a separately supplied self-adjoint realization of the\neffective generator $G=H-\\mu\\mathcal N$, where $\\mathcal N$ is the declared\nparticle-number operator and $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$ is an assigned chemical\npotential in joules. The subtraction is an operator with its supplied domain,\nnot an assertion that a formal unbounded difference is self-adjoint.\n\nIn either reading assume $G$ is bounded below and give its spectral PVM\n$E_G$ ([[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]],\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]]). Let $T>0$ be an assigned temperature in\nkelvin and let $\\beta:=1/(k_BT)$ in $\\mathrm J^{-1}$ with the assigned\nBoltzmann constant $k_B$. The Gibbs assignment to either model, and the\nchemical-potential identification in (ii), are additional physical\npreparation assumptions made here; they are not derived from P1-P4 or from\nthe fixed-$N$ construction. In (ii) no restriction on the assigned real\n$\\mu$ is imposed beyond the declared generator and trace existence\nconditions; the mode-specific convergence conditions are proved separately.\nFor reading (ii), $G$ specifies the Gibbs weights. If the assigned autonomous\ndynamics is generated by $H$, stationarity of the Gibbs density requires its\nseparate dynamical or commutation hypotheses; this assignment does not\nidentify $H-\\mu\\mathcal N$ with the physical evolution generator.\nThe physical Helmholtz free-energy vocabulary of the entropy definition\ncontinues to apply only to its stated nonzero finite-sector class and is not\nextended to every effective generator by this postulate.\nAssume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), the choice hypotheses of\nthe spectral calculus.\n\n**(b) Existence hypothesis and the Gibbs preparation.** The model assumes\nthat the **partition trace is strictly positive and finite**:\n$$0<Z:=\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-\\beta G}<\\infty,$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Tr}B:=\\sum_{f\\in\\mathcal B}\\langle Bf,f\\rangle$ for a\nbounded positive $B$ and an orthonormal basis $\\mathcal B$, with value allowed\nto be $+\\infty$. Such a basis exists under the declared AC by the maximal\northonormal-family argument using [[thm-zorn]] and the zero-complement\ncriterion of [[thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family]].\nThis trace is basis independent: for $C:=B^{1/2}$ of\n[[thm-positive-square-root]], the defining sum is\n$\\sum_{f\\in\\mathcal B}\\|Cf\\|^2$, whose invariance is\n[[thm-hilbert-schmidt-norm-is-basis-independent]]. When the sum is finite,\n$C$ is Hilbert-Schmidt ([[def-hilbert-schmidt-operator]]), so $B=C^2$ is\ntrace class by [[thm-trace-class-iff-product-of-two-hilbert-schmidt-operators]],\nand its ordinary trace is this same diagonal sum by\n[[thm-trace-is-absolutely-convergent-and-basis-independent]]. Apply this to the bounded positive operator $e^{-\\beta G}$, since the\ndeclared Gibbs generator $G$ is bounded below. The model\nadopts as the **canonical** or **Gibbs preparation** of the declared model\nat temperature $T$ the density operator\n$$\\rho_\\beta:=Z^{-1}e^{-\\beta G}.$$\nIf the sector is zero-dimensional, its partition trace is $Z=0$, no\ntrace-one density exists, and no canonical density or equilibrium free\nenergy is assigned. On a nonzero finite-dimensional model space, $Z$ is a\npositive finite sum $\\sum_je^{-\\beta E_j}$ over the eigenvalues of $G$; no energy\nsign restriction is needed. There $\\rho_\\beta$ is the canonical operator of\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-finite-gibbs-free-energy-minimum]], and the\nstrict positivity and finiteness hypothesis is automatic. In reading (i),\n$Z=Z_N$ and this is precisely the finite-sector Gibbs assignment.\n\nOutside the finite-dimensional class the existence hypothesis is a genuine\nrestriction. For a declared non-interacting fixed-$N$ model with a supplied\ncountably infinite orthonormal eigenmode basis and real mode energies\n$\\varepsilon_j$ bounded below, the complete construction and trace criterion\nof [[lem-nrqm-many-body-fixed-n-countable-mode-partition-criterion]] give\n$Z_N=h_N(q)$ for bosonic statistics or $Z_N=e_N(q)$ for fermionic statistics,\nwhere $q_j=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j}$ and $h_N,e_N$ are the corresponding\nnonnegative occupation-series sums. For every fixed $N\\ge1$, either trace is\nfinite exactly when $\\sum_jq_j<\\infty$. This is a trace on the fixed-$N$\nsector and permits negative mode energies.\n\nFor the separately declared full bosonic Fock models of reading (ii),\nthe single-mode calculation of\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-single-mode-thermal-trace]] and the product\ncriterion of [[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-countable-mode-product-criterion]]\nprovide the separate mathematical full-Fock traces, with their original\npositive mode-energy hypotheses: they sum over every particle number,\nincluding the vacuum, giving $(1-e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon})^{-1}$ for one mode\nand $\\prod_j(1-e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j})^{-1}$ for countably many modes.\nTheir physical Gibbs reading is the additional assignment of (a)(ii), not a\nconsequence of a mathematical trace formula. Those full-Fock traces are not\nsubstituted for the fixed-$N$ partition trace of reading (i).\n\n**(c) The equilibrium assumption and thermal means.** The model **assumes**\nthat, for a declared sample of the species at the assigned temperature $T$\nand in the absence of further preparation information, the preparation\ndescription of the sample is $\\rho_\\beta$ in the sense of P1. For an assigned\nsharp observable $(A,u_A)$ with spectral PVM $E_A$, define its outcome law\n$\\mu_{\\rho_\\beta}^A(B):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_\\beta E_A(B))$ as in\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] clause (b).\nIf and only if the absolute first moment is finite,\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R}|a|\\,d\\mu_{\\rho_\\beta}^A(a)<\\infty,$$\nthe model defines the **thermal mean** at temperature $T$ by\n$$\\langle A\\rangle_\\beta:=\\int_{\\mathbb R}a\\,d\\mu_{\\rho_\\beta}^A(a),$$\nread in the assigned unit $u_A$. For bounded $A$ this equals\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_\\beta A)=\\operatorname{tr}(A\\rho_\\beta)$. For\nunbounded $A$, if the mean exists and the literal product $A\\rho_\\beta$ is\ndefined on the whole Hilbert space and is trace class, the mean also equals\n$\\operatorname{tr}(A\\rho_\\beta)$ and\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\overline{\\rho_\\beta A})$; these conditional product\nidentities are proved by the density eigenexpansion in\n[[lem-nrqm-many-body-spectral-mean-and-defined-trace-product]]. First-moment\nexistence alone imposes no such product or operator-square-domain condition.\nWhen the absolute first moment is infinite the model asserts no finite\nthermal mean. The identification of the parameter $T$ with the thermodynamic temperature of a\nsample, and of $\\beta$ with its inverse temperature, is part of this\nassumption; the stated conservation and equilibrium properties of\n$\\rho_\\beta$ under the flow of P4, and the extremal property of $\\rho_\\beta$\namong preparations, are separate conditional results of this pair.\n\n**(d) Motivation and status.** The assumption is motivated, but not\nestablished, by the maximum-entropy reasoning of the cited sources: among\npreparations with a prescribed mean energy, the Gibbs weights maximize the\nentropy functional, and the finite-dimensional comparison theorem makes\n$\\rho_\\beta$ the minimizer of the corresponding functional built from $G$.\nIts physical Helmholtz free-energy reading applies in the stated finite\nfixed-sector class; an effective-generator interpretation in a different\nmode model is a separate declared reading. That reasoning is\nan argument inside mathematical statistics and thermodynamics; it does not\nderive the physical assumption that a sample is described by $\\rho_\\beta$.\nThe postulate is an **adopted** statistical-mechanical assumption of the\nmodel, in the sense of the content model's distinction between adopted\nassumptions and derived consequences.\n\n**(e) Scope.** The postulate asserts nothing about how a sample reaches the\nGibbs preparation; in particular it asserts no thermalization, no equilibration\ntime, no ergodicity, no master-equation or Markov approximation, no\nthermodynamic limit, no pressure, volume or phase-transition structure, and\nno measured temperature or expectation. The requirement $0<Z<\\infty$ is part\nof the assumption; the model does not define a canonical preparation when\n$Z=0$ or the partition trace diverges. Any comparison with a laboratory sample requires the\ndeclared preparation, isolation, detection and sampling models of P7, and\nagreement of a thermal prediction with data is evidence about the combined\nmodel and apparatus assumptions, not a proof of the framework or of this\npostulate.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-single-mode-thermal-trace",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice\n([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $\\omega>0$ and\n$\\beta>0$ be real parameters, let $F(\\mathbb C)$ be the one-mode Fock space\nwith its complete orthonormal family $(e_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ and number\noperator $N$ as in [[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fixed-mode-number-law]]\nand [[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-number-operator-domain]], and define the\noperator $G_\\beta\\in\\mathcal B(F(\\mathbb C))$ by\n$$G_\\beta e_n:=e^{-\\beta\\omega n}e_n\\qquad(n\\in\\mathbb N)$$\ntogether with bounded linear extension. Then:\n\n(a) $G_\\beta$ is a bounded self-adjoint positive operator with $\\|G_\\beta\\|=1$,\nand $G_\\beta=\\bigoplus_{n\\in\\mathbb N}e^{-\\beta\\omega n}\\,\\mathrm{id}$ on the\nFock sectors ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-operator-norm]]);\n\n(b) the series of weights converge,\n$$Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb N}e^{-\\beta\\omega n}=\\frac1{1-e^{-\\beta\\omega}},\\qquad \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb N}n\\,e^{-\\beta\\omega n}=\\frac{e^{-\\beta\\omega}}{(1-e^{-\\beta\\omega})^2};$$\n\n(c) $G_\\beta$ is trace class with\n$\\|G_\\beta\\|_1=\\operatorname{tr}G_\\beta=Z(\\beta)$\n([[def-trace-class-operator]], [[def-trace-of-a-trace-class-operator]]);\n\n(d) $\\rho_\\beta:=G_\\beta/Z(\\beta)$ is a density operator on $F(\\mathbb C)$\n([[lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability]]), and its\nnumber expectation, defined by the absolutely convergent spectral sum\n$\\langle N\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_{n}n\\,e^{-\\beta\\omega n}/Z(\\beta)$, equals\n$$\\langle N\\rangle_\\beta=\\frac{e^{-\\beta\\omega}}{1-e^{-\\beta\\omega}}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\omega}-1};$$\nmore generally $\\sum_nn^k e^{-\\beta\\omega n}<\\infty$ for every $k\\ge1$.\n\nThe parameter $\\beta$ is a positive real parameter of the model; its\nidentification with an inverse temperature, and the interpretation of\n$\\rho_\\beta$ as a physical thermal state, are additional physical assumptions\nnot asserted here.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-many-body-two-level-thermal-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$\\varepsilon>0$ and $\\beta>0$ be real parameters, let $\\mathfrak h$ be a\ntwo-dimensional complex Hilbert space with orthonormal basis\n$(u_0,u_1)$ ([[def-hilbert-space]],\n[[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]),\nand let $H:=T_{u_1,u_1}\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon\\,T_{u_1,u_1}$ be the\nself-adjoint operator with eigenvalues $0$ on $\\mathbb Cu_0$ and\n$\\varepsilon$ on $\\mathbb Cu_1$\n([[lem-nrqm-composites-rank-one-operator-calculus]]). Let\n$$Z:=\\sum_{j=0}^{1}e^{-\\beta E_j},\\qquad \\rho_\\beta:=\\frac1Z\\sum_{j=0}^{1}e^{-\\beta E_j}T_{u_j,u_j},\\qquad s(\\rho):=-\\sum_{i:\\,w_i>0}w_i\\log w_i$$\nbe the partition sum, canonical operator and entropy of\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-finite-gibbs-free-energy-minimum]] clause\n(a), with $E_0=0$, $E_1=\\varepsilon$ and $w_i$ the eigenvalues of a density\noperator $\\rho$ on $\\mathfrak h$ ([[def-natural-logarithm]]). Then:\n\n(a) $Z=1+e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}\\in(1,2)$, and\n$$\\rho_\\beta=\\frac1Z\\bigl(T_{u_0,u_0}+e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}T_{u_1,u_1}\\bigr)$$\nis a density operator on $\\mathfrak h$ that is not the maximally mixed state\n$\\tfrac12\\mathrm{id}$;\n\n(b) the mean energy is the exact identity\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_\\beta H)=\\frac{\\varepsilon e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}}{Z}=\\frac{\\varepsilon}{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}+1},\\qquad 0<\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_\\beta H)<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2};$$\n\n(c) the entropy and free energy satisfy\n$$s(\\rho_\\beta)=\\log Z+\\frac{\\beta\\varepsilon}{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}+1}=\\log\\bigl(1+e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}\\bigr)+\\frac{\\beta\\varepsilon\\,e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}}{1+e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}},\\qquad 0\\le s(\\rho_\\beta)<\\log 2,$$\nand, with $F:=-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z$,\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_\\beta H)-\\beta^{-1}s(\\rho_\\beta)=F ;$$\n\n(d) *scope*: clauses (a)-(c) are exact algebraic identities for the\ndeclared two-dimensional Hilbert space and the two real parameters\n$\\varepsilon,\\beta>0$; no temperature, thermodynamic, equilibrium or\nmeasurement interpretation is asserted, and the parameter $\\beta$ carries no\nphysical dimension in this lemma.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "pthm-nrqm-many-body-thermal-occupation-predictions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the canonical equilibrium-ensemble postulate\n[[post-nrqm-many-body-canonical-equilibrium-ensemble]] for the mode models\ndeclared below, the state and probability postulates P1, P2\n([[post-nrqm-framework-state-and-composition]],\n[[post-nrqm-framework-observable-and-probability]]) in the readings of\n[[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] and\n[[pthm-nrqm-observables-preparation-moments]], and the\nidentical-particle and spin postulate P6\n([[post-nrqm-framework-identical-particles-and-spin]]); assume the Axiom of\nCountable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]],\n[[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Temperatures are assigned as in\n[[def-nrqm-many-body-framework-entropy-and-free-energy]], so that\n$\\beta=1/(k_BT)$ in $\\mathrm J^{-1}$. Declare the occupation readouts as assigned sharp observables $(N,1)$ and $(N_-,1)$, with unit particle-count, which is dimensionless. The total particle-number operator $\\mathcal N$ in the countable-mode reading has the same unit; every occupation mean and higher number moment below is read in the corresponding dimensionless count unit. Then:\n\n(a) *single bosonic mode*: let $F(\\mathbb C)$ be the one-mode Fock space with\nnumber operator $N$ and orthonormal number basis $(e_n)$\n([[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-fixed-mode-number-law]],\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-number-operator-domain]]), let\n$\\varepsilon>0$ and let the model declare the energy operator $H:=\\varepsilon N$\non $F(\\mathbb C)$; the model's canonical preparation at temperature $T$ is\n$\\rho_\\beta=e^{-\\beta H}/Z$ with $Z=(1-e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon})^{-1}$, the\nnumber readout $N$ is an assigned sharp observable with finite thermal mean\n$$\\langle N\\rangle_\\beta=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}-1}=\\frac1{e^{\\varepsilon/(k_BT)}-1},$$\nand all moments $\\langle N^k\\rangle_\\beta$, $k\\ge1$, are finite;\n\n(b) *single fermionic orbital and grand-canonical shift*: on\n$\\mathfrak h=\\mathbb C^2$ with occupation operator $N_-$ and\n$\\varepsilon_1,\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$, the model's canonical (grand-canonical)\npreparation $F=Z^{-1}\\sum_{k=0}^1e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)k}T_{u_k,u_k}$\nhas $Z=1+e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)}$, $\\operatorname{tr}F=1$, and occupation mean\n$$\\langle N_-\\rangle=\\frac1{e^{\\beta(\\varepsilon_1-\\mu)}+1};$$\nthe same formula is the model's thermal mean when $\\mu=0$ and\n$\\varepsilon_1>0$;\n\n(c) *countably many independent modes*: let $(\\varepsilon_j)_{j\\ge1}\\subseteq(0,\\infty)$\nand $\\mu\\in\\mathbb R$, and let the model declare the free-mode energies\n$\\varepsilon_j$ (one mode case of clause (a)) with the product reading of\n[[thm-nrqm-fock-and-equilibrium-countable-mode-product-criterion]]; then the\nbosonic product partition is finite exactly when\n$\\sum_je^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j}<\\infty$ and $\\mu<\\inf_j\\varepsilon_j$, and\nthe fermionic product partition is finite exactly when\n$\\sum_je^{-\\beta\\varepsilon_j}<\\infty$; in the finite cases the products\nequal $\\prod_j(1-e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_j-\\mu)})^{-1}$ and\n$\\prod_j(1+e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon_j-\\mu)})$ respectively;\n\n(d) *dilute remainders*: for $q=e^{-\\beta(\\varepsilon-\\mu)}$ the model's\noccupation means satisfy the exact identities\n$$\\frac q{1-q}=q+\\frac{q^2}{1-q}\\quad(\\text{bosonic},\\ 0<q<1),\\qquad \\frac q{1+q}=q-\\frac{q^2}{1+q}\\quad(\\text{fermionic},\\ q>0),$$\nso both differ from $q$ by at most $q^2/(1-q)$ and $q^2/(1+q)$\nrespectively;\n\n(e) *scope*: clauses (a)-(d) are conditional statements about the declared\nmode models together with the adopted ensemble assignment; they assert no\nthermodynamic limit, no continuum of radiation modes, no Bose-Einstein\ncondensation, no measured occupation number, and no thermalisation of a\nsample. All numerical equalities are exact identities of the declared\nfinite-mode (or product) models at the assigned temperature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "pthm-td-independent-factorization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let two finite microstate models from [[def-td-finite-microstate-model]] have nonempty state sets $\\Omega_A,\\Omega_B$, energies $E_A,E_B$ in joules, and the same $T>0$. Assume their declared composite model has state set $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$ and energy $E_{AB}(x,y)=E_A(x)+E_B(y)$, with no interaction term. Under the canonical sampling assumption [[post-td-canonical-sampling]], the partition function factorizes as $Z_{AB}=Z_AZ_B$, the joint canonical law is $P_{AB}=P_AP_B$, the Gibbs entropy satisfies $S_{AB}=S_A+S_B$, and the statistical free-energy value satisfies $F_{AB}=F_A+F_B$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m³); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Dimensions.** Write $\\ell$, $\\tau$, $\\mu$, $\\vartheta$ for the four independent dimension symbols of length, duration, mass and electric charge. A **dimension** is an element of $\\mathbb Q^{4}$, written $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\vartheta^{d}$ with $a,b,c,d\\in\\mathbb Q$; products and inverses of dimensions are componentwise addition and negation, and the dimensionless dimension $\\ell^{0}\\tau^{0}\\mu^{0}\\vartheta^{0}$ is abbreviated $1$. The conventions assign to each quantity of the framework a dimension $[X]$, subject to $[XY]=[X][Y]$, $[X/Y]=[X][Y]^{-1}$ and $[dX/du]=[X][u]^{-1}$, with sums meaningful only between quantities of equal dimension, in which case the sum carries that dimension. For a rational power $q$, dimension powers mean $[X]^q=\\ell^{qa}\\tau^{qb}\\mu^{qc}\\vartheta^{qd}$ when $[X]=\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\vartheta^{d}$. Rational exponents include the half-integer wave-function dimensions in clause (d); the integer-exponent dimensions form the subgroup $\\mathbb Z^4$. These are consistency requirements on the conventions, not laws of nature.\n\nFor extensions using temperature, adjoin an independent dimension symbol $\\Theta$: the enlarged group is $\\mathbb Q^4\\times\\mathbb Q$, written $\\ell^a\\tau^b\\mu^c\\vartheta^d\\Theta^e$, with the same componentwise rules. The preceding group embeds by $e=0$, so all previously assigned dimensions are preserved. Temperature has dimension $\\Theta$, the Boltzmann constant has dimension $\\mu\\ell^2\\tau^{-2}\\Theta^{-1}$, and entropy in joules per kelvin has that same dimension; a dimensionless entropy remains dimensionless. This convention does not assign a temperature or an entropy to any system.\n\n**(b) Reference units.** The **metre** (m), **second** (s), **kilogram** (kg) and **coulomb** (C) are the reference units of length, duration, mass and charge; a quantity of dimension $\\ell^{a}\\tau^{b}\\mu^{c}\\vartheta^{d}$ is expressed in the unit $\\mathrm m^{a}\\mathrm s^{b}\\mathrm kg^{c}\\mathrm C^{d}$. Coordinates of an inertial frame are read with the time coordinate $t$ in seconds, $[t]=\\tau$, and the spatial coordinates $x_{i}$ in metres, $[x_{i}]=\\ell$; slice distances of the arena are read in metres. In the temperature extension the reference unit of $\\Theta$ is the kelvin (K), so the general reference unit gains a factor $\\mathrm K^e$.\n\n**(c) Defining constants of the current SI.** The framework keeps $\\hbar$ explicit. In the current SI the constants $h$, $c$ and $e$ are exact defining constants, with the values $h=6{,}626\\,070\\,15\\times10^{-34}$ J s, $c=2{,}99792458\\times10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$ and $e=1{,}602\\,176\\,634\\times10^{-19}$ C, and $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. These are real scalar constants with dimensions and units $[h]=[\\hbar]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$ (J s), $[c]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ (m s$^{-1}$), and $[e]=\\vartheta$ (C). The vacuum permeability $\\mu_{0}$ is a positive real scalar that is experimentally determined in the current SI, with $[\\mu_{0}]=\\mu\\ell\\vartheta^{-2}$ (N A$^{-2}$ = H m$^{-1}$); $\\varepsilon_{0}:=(\\mu_{0}c^{2})^{-1}$ is a positive real scalar with $[\\varepsilon_{0}]=\\mu^{-1}\\ell^{-3}\\tau^{2}\\vartheta^{2}$ (F m$^{-1}$). These are unit-convention statements of the SI, not measurements reported by this framework; $c$ enters the framework only through the separate radiation assumptions adopted on their own pages, not in the nonrelativistic spacetime of [[def-nrqm-framework-newtonian-spacetime]].\n\n**(d) Dimensions and types of the framework's quantities.** The dimension map is fixed on the following quantities.\n\n1. State quantities: a position wave function of a system with $d$ spatial dimensions and $r$ internal components is an almost-everywhere equivalence class in $L^{2}(\\mathbb R^{d};\\mathbb C^{r})$ with dimension $\\ell^{-d/2}$, its norm $\\|\\psi\\|$ is dimensionless, and an abstract normalized state and the density operators of the state postulate are dimensionless.\n2. Probability: the outcome probability $\\mu(B)$ of the observable postulate is a dimensionless number; a position density $|\\psi|^{2}$ has dimension $\\ell^{-d}$; a momentum wave function normalized against $\\mathrm d^{d}p$ has dimension $(\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1})^{-d/2}$; an amplitude for a finite outcome is a dimensionless complex number, while an amplitude read against a detector coordinate carries the square-root of the inverse of the detector measure.\n3. Frame parameters ([[def-nrqm-framework-galilei-transformations]]): $[b]=\\tau$, $[a_{i}]=\\ell$, $[v_{i}]=\\ell\\tau^{-1}$ and $[R_{ij}]=1$.\n4. Mechanical quantities: action $[\\hbar]=\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$; energy and the Hamiltonian eigenvalues $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$; momentum $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-1}$; position components $\\ell$; angular momentum and spin $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-1}$; mass $\\mu$; velocity of a free spinless model $\\ell\\tau^{-1}$; force $\\mu\\ell\\tau^{-2}$; a real potential energy $\\mu\\ell^{2}\\tau^{-2}$.\n5. Probability current: in the ordinary continuous-field regime, fix an integer $d\\ge1$, an open spatial region $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ and an open time interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$. A **probability current density** is a declared continuous map $j:\\Omega\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^d$, whose spatial components have dimension $\\ell^{1-d}\\tau^{-1}$ and reference unit $\\mathrm m^{1-d}\\mathrm s^{-1}$. At fixed time it is a spatial vector field; its signed normal component is read as probability transported per unit spatial $(d-1)$-area and time. Explicitly, for a flat face $S=\\{x:x_k=a,\\ a_i\\le x_i\\le b_i\\ (i\\ne k)\\}\\subset\\Omega$, with $a_i<b_i$ and chosen normal $n=\\epsilon e_k$, $\\epsilon\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, define its **signed probability flux** at $t\\in I$ as $\\Phi_S(t):=\\epsilon\\int_{\\prod_{i\\ne k}[a_i,b_i]}j_k(x_1,\\ldots,x_{k-1},a,x_{k+1},\\ldots,x_d,t)\\prod_{i\\ne k}dx_i$. Here $e_k$ is the $k$th coordinate unit vector and the integral uses $(d-1)$-dimensional Lebesgue measure; for $d=1$ this means $\\Phi_{\\{a\\}}(t)=\\epsilon j(a,t)$. The flux has dimension $\\tau^{-1}$ and unit $\\mathrm s^{-1}$; positive sign means transport in the chosen normal direction. Fluxes through curved surfaces, or currents with weaker regularity, require separately specified surface measures or traces. This fixes the quantity type and flux convention; it supplies neither a current associated to an arbitrary $L^2$ state nor a conservation or detector law. Each dynamical model supplies its current construction and balance hypotheses.\n6. Electric quantities of the model parameters: charge has dimension $\\vartheta$; a magnetic moment has dimension $\\ell^{2}\\vartheta\\tau^{-1}$, equivalently $\\mathrm A\\,\\mathrm m^{2}=\\mathrm J\\,\\mathrm T^{-1}$, and a gyromagnetic parameter $\\gamma$ in the relation $\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\gamma\\mathbf S$ has dimension $\\mu^{-1}\\vartheta$, equivalently $\\mathrm s^{-1}\\mathrm T^{-1}$; these quantities appear only in the model assumptions that introduce them.\n7. Statistical quantities: a mean has the dimension of its outcome coordinate, a variance the square of it and a standard deviation it; a mean, variance or higher moment exists only when the corresponding moment of the outcome probability is finite, so a symbolic average is not a number unless that integrability is established. Angles and phases are dimensionless under the radian convention.\n8. Quantities of later extension assumptions - radiation frequencies and energies, electric dipole moments, cross sections, temperatures and entropies - are declared with their dimensions and units by the items that introduce those assumptions, under the rules fixed here; they are not part of the present list.\n\n**(e) Conventions only.** No numerical value of any model parameter is fixed by this item: no mass, charge, spin label, potential or measured constant is asserted, and natural-unit shorthand (for example setting $\\hbar=1$ or $c=1$) is read as an abbreviation for the corresponding SI-dimensioned statement with the constant restored.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For one bosonic mode, [F2] gives $Z_B=(1-q)^{-1}$ and $\\langle N\\rangle_B=q/(1-q)$; for $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}$, multiplication of numerator and denominator by $e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}$ gives $q/(1-q)=1/(e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}-1)$. The unit convention in [F6] makes the exponent dimensionless.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For one fermionic occupation, [F3] gives $Z_F=1+q$ and upper-level probability $q/(1+q)$; multiplying numerator and denominator by $e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}$ gives $q/(1+q)=1/(e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}+1)$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a finite independent collection, apply [F5] repeatedly to factor the full partition trace into the product of its one-mode factors. Summing the product law over all other mode labels leaves each single-mode marginal unchanged, so the means in steps 1.1–1.2 apply componentwise.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Adopt the quantum Gibbs preparation of [[post-nrqm-many-body-canonical-equilibrium-ensemble]] for a finite list of independent modes at $T>0$. Boundary empty: The statement explicitly requires a nonempty state set, nonzero sector, nonempty shell, or positive trace; no empty-model conclusion is claimed.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Statement",
            "source_excerpt": "Adopt the quantum Gibbs preparation of [[post-nrqm-many-body-canonical-equilibrium-ensemble]] for a finite list of independent modes at $T>0$. For a bosonic mode with excitation energy $\\varepsilon>0$ joules, let $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}$, $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$, and let $N$ be its dimensionless occupation number. Its partition trace and mean occupation are\n$$Z_B=(1-q)^{-1},\\qquad \\langle N\\rangle_B=\\frac{q}{1-q}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}-1}.$$\nFor a fermionic two-level occupation system with energies $0,\\varepsilon$, the partition sum and excited-state occupation are\n$$Z_F=1+q,\\qquad \\langle N\\rangle_F=\\frac{q}{1+q}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}+1}.$$\nFor a finite independent collection, the corresponding partition traces multiply and each mode's marginal mean is the displayed one. These are exact model identities under the adopted preparation.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "249423a6b17acc8015579f723c0d25a95da33398ba11c4e846dee77dd0e2cde3",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
