    "ex-td-infinite-mode-trace-divergence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-finite-mode-occupation-and-trace-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the quantum Gibbs preparation of [[post-nrqm-many-body-canonical-equilibrium-ensemble]] for a finite list of independent modes at $T>0$. For a bosonic mode with excitation energy $\\varepsilon>0$ joules, let $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}$, $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$, and let $N$ be its dimensionless occupation number. Its partition trace and mean occupation are\n$$Z_B=(1-q)^{-1},\\qquad \\langle N\\rangle_B=\\frac{q}{1-q}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}-1}.$$\nFor a fermionic two-level occupation system with energies $0,\\varepsilon$, the partition sum and excited-state occupation are\n$$Z_F=1+q,\\qquad \\langle N\\rangle_F=\\frac{q}{1+q}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}+1}.$$\nFor a finite independent collection, the corresponding partition traces multiply and each mode's marginal mean is the displayed one. These are exact model identities under the adopted preparation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "post-td-canonical-sampling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-trace-class-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ and\n$K$ be Hilbert spaces over the same field $\\mathbb F\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$\n([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H,K)$ be a compact operator ([[def-compact-linear-operator]])\nwith zero-padded singular-value sequence $(s_n(T))_{n\\ge1}$\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]).\n\n**Trace class.** The operator $T$ is **trace class** when\n$$\\sum_{n\\ge1}s_n(T):=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb N}s_{m+1}(T)<+\\infty .$$\nThus the series in the zero-based convention of\n[[def-series-and-absolute-convergence-in-a-normed-space]] is formed from the\nexplicit sequence $(s_{m+1}(T))_{m\\in\\mathbb N}$. In that case its **trace\nnorm** is\n$$\\|T\\|_1:=\\sum_{m\\in\\mathbb N}s_{m+1}(T)\\in[0,+\\infty),$$\nand $\\|T\\|_1$ is also written $\\|T\\|_{\\mathrm{tr}}$. The set of trace-class\noperators $H\\to K$ is written $\\mathcal S_1(H,K)$.\n\n**Immediate consequences.** Since $0\\le s_n(T)\\le s_1(T)=\\|T\\|$\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]), the terms\nare nonnegative and $\\|T\\|_1\\ge\\|T\\|\\ge0$ whenever $T$ is trace class; the zero\noperator is trace class with $\\|0\\|_1=0$. When $T$ has finite rank $r$ the\nsequence is zero-padded, the series is the finite sum\n$\\sum_{n=1}^r s_n(T)=\\sum_{\\lambda>0}\\lambda\\dim_{\\mathbb F} E_\\lambda(|T|)$ over the finitely\nmany positive eigenvalues of $|T|$ counted with multiplicity ([[def-dimension]]),\nand every finite-rank operator is compact\n([[lem-finite-rank-operators-are-compact]]) and therefore trace class; in particular every\noperator with finite-dimensional range and every rank-one operator is trace\nclass. If $T$ has infinite rank then $s_n(T)>0$ for every $n$ and the series\n$\\sum_{n\\ge1}s_n(T)$ converges in the summable case. By the necessary\ncondition for convergence of a scalar series ([[lem-nth-term-test]]), a\ntrace-class operator with infinite rank has $s_n(T)\\to0$, hence\nis a norm limit of finite-rank operators\n([[thm-singular-value-decomposition-for-compact-operators]]).\n\n**Choice accounting.** Trace class is defined through the singular values of\n[[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]], whose\nconstruction uses $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ through the countable selection of finite\northonormal bases of the eigenspaces of $|T|$ and the spectral theorem; no\nHilbert basis of the ambient space, and no stronger choice, is used here.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-trace-of-a-positive-operator-is-the-sum-of-its-eigenvalues",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\nreal or complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be a **self-adjoint positive** trace-class operator\n([[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]],\n[[def-trace-class-operator]]), so that $\\langle Tx,x\\rangle\\ge0$ for every\n$x\\in H$. Let $\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots>0$ be the positive eigenvalues of\n$T$ listed with multiplicity\n([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]]) and let\n$(s_n(T))_{n\\ge1}$ be the zero-padded singular-value sequence\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]). Then\n$s_n(T)=\\lambda_n$ for every $n$, and\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(T)=\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\lambda_n=\\|T\\|_1 ,$$\nwhere the sum is over the positive eigenvalues with multiplicity and the\nequalities are also valid in the finite-rank case (then $\\lambda_n:=0$ for\n$n$ beyond the rank). This is the positive compact self-adjoint case only; it is\n**not** Lidskii's theorem for arbitrary trace-class operators, which is not\nclaimed here.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $I$ be a nonempty finite or countably infinite set equipped with a specified enumeration, let $E_i\\in\\mathbb R$ be finite energies, let $g_i>0$ be weights, and let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open parameter interval. For a nonnegative family $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$ define $\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i:=\\sup_{F\\subset I,\\,F\\text{ finite}}\\sum_{i\\in F}a_i$, with finite sums as in [[def-finite-sum]] and the supremum as in [[def-complete-ordered-field]] when the finite subsums are bounded; set the value to $+\\infty$ when they are unbounded. This definition is independent of the specified enumeration. For $\\beta\\in J$, define the nonnegative partition sum $Z(\\beta):=\\sum_{i\\in I}g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)$ by this rule. The admissible parameter set is $J_Z:=\\{\\beta\\in J:0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty\\}$. For $\\beta\\in J_Z$, define $p_i(\\beta):=g_i\\exp(-\\beta E_i)/Z(\\beta)$; then $(p_i)$ is a countable probability law. For any real observable $X_i$ with $\\sum_i p_i|X_i|<\\infty$, define $\\langle X\\rangle_\\beta:=\\sum_i p_iX_i$. If $\\sum_i|p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)|<\\infty$, define the reference-relative entropy $H_g(p):=-\\sum_i p_i\\log(p_i/g_i)$. When $g_i=1$, this is the Shannon entropy with natural logarithms. A lower bound on the energies alone is not part of this definition and does not imply $Z(\\beta)<\\infty$. For countably infinite $I$, any specified enumeration is used only to write a list; the nonnegative sum itself is the supremum over finite subsums above.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-td-si-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "This framework uses the modern International System of Units (SI). A scalar quantity has a real numerical value with the stated unit; a vector quantity has a value in the stated real coordinate space with the stated component unit. The numerical coordinates used elsewhere on this page are divided by their displayed SI units before they are placed in a Euclidean coordinate space.\n\nThe model's energy coordinate $U$ and its process-transfer values $Q$ and $W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are scalar energies measured in joules (J). Volume $V$ is a scalar measured in cubic metres (m³); amount of substance $n_j$ for species $j$ is a scalar measured in moles (mol); and a particle count $N_j$, when separately used in a microscopic model, is dimensionless. Time $t$ is measured in seconds (s), mass $M$ in kilograms (kg), and a translational velocity $\\mathbf v_{\\mathrm{cm}}$ is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ with components measured in metres per second (m/s). The physical meanings of $U,Q,W_{\\mathrm{on}}$ are fixed by the state and process definitions, not by their units alone.\n\nThe relevant exact SI relations are\n\n$$1\\,\\mathrm J=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm N=1\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-2}},\\qquad 1\\,\\mathrm{Pa}=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}.$$\n\nThe kelvin (K) is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. The Boltzmann constant has the exact defining value $k_{\\mathrm B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. The mole is the SI unit of amount of substance; the Avogadro constant has the exact defining value $N_{\\mathrm A}=6.02214076\\times10^{23}\\,\\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$. When a model uses particle counts, $n_j=N_j/N_{\\mathrm A}$. The molar gas constant is derived as $R=N_{\\mathrm A}k_{\\mathrm B}=8.31446261815324\\,\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. This framework does not set $k_{\\mathrm B}=1$. These SI definitions do not themselves introduce a thermodynamic temperature state function or entropy function.\n\nWithin the macroscopic framework, $(U,V,n_j)$ are adopted state coordinates. A quantity's status as primitive in this model is separate from whether its SI unit is a base or derived unit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The trace contribution from states supported on the first $M$ modes is the finite product $Z_M=(1-q)^{-M}$ by [F1]. Since $c=(1-q)^{-1}>1$, $Z_M=c^M$ is unbounded: writing $c=1+r$ with $r>0$, induction gives $(1+r)^M\\ge1+Mr$. The unit convention [F5] makes $\\beta\\varepsilon$ dimensionless.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $K_\\beta$ were trace class, [F3] would make its positive eigenvalue sum finite and equal to its trace. For each $M$, the configurations with $n_j=0$ for $j>M$ form a subset of the enumerated states [A1]; [F4] therefore makes their finite-mode partition sum $Z_M$ no larger than the full normalizer. [F1] identifies that subset sum as $Z_M$, and step 1.1 makes $Z_M$ unbounded, a contradiction. Hence $K_\\beta$ is not trace class and the countable partition sum has no finite value. The preparation condition [F2] fails, so this model has no normalized countable canonical law of the stated form.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Take modes indexed by $j\\in\\mathbb N$, each with excitation energy $\\varepsilon>0$, and let $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}\\in(0,1)$. Boundary empty: The statement explicitly requires a nonempty state set, nonzero sector, nonempty shell, or positive trace; no empty-model conclusion is claimed.",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "Take modes indexed by $j\\in\\mathbb N$, each with excitation energy $\\varepsilon>0$, and let $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}\\in(0,1)$. Let $\\mathcal F$ be the set of finite-support occupation sequences $n=(n_j)_{j\\ge1}$ with $n_j\\in\\mathbb N_0$. Fix its enumeration by increasing $R(n)=\\sum_{j:n_j>0}(j+n_j)$, breaking ties lexicographically by the finite list of nonzero pairs $(j,n_j)$; for each bound on $R$, only finitely many configurations occur. On $\\ell^2(\\mathcal F)$ with its occupation basis $|n\\rangle$, define the bounded positive diagonal Gibbs weight $K_\\beta|n\\rangle=q^{\\sum_jn_j}|n\\rangle$. On the subspace with $n_j=0$ for every $j>M$, the corresponding finite-mode partition sum is the trace of that restriction.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "b40f91d64374285ad1e9151585d8c7b96b84a240af62b134b5998333f75286c4",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
        },
        {
