        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement/Example scope: Take modes indexed by $j\\in\\mathbb N$, each with excitation energy $\\varepsilon>0$, and let $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}\\in(0,1)$. Case degenerate: No nondegeneracy assumption is used; equal energy labels remain distinct states or are handled by the stated full energy blocks (Statement).",
          "case_scope": {
            "source_section": "Example",
            "source_excerpt": "Take modes indexed by $j\\in\\mathbb N$, each with excitation energy $\\varepsilon>0$, and let $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}\\in(0,1)$. Let $\\mathcal F$ be the set of finite-support occupation sequences $n=(n_j)_{j\\ge1}$ with $n_j\\in\\mathbb N_0$. Fix its enumeration by increasing $R(n)=\\sum_{j:n_j>0}(j+n_j)$, breaking ties lexicographically by the finite list of nonzero pairs $(j,n_j)$; for each bound on $R$, only finitely many configurations occur. On $\\ell^2(\\mathcal F)$ with its occupation basis $|n\\rangle$, define the bounded positive diagonal Gibbs weight $K_\\beta|n\\rangle=q^{\\sum_jn_j}|n\\rangle$. On the subspace with $n_j=0$ for every $j>M$, the corresponding finite-mode partition sum is the trace of that restriction.",
            "current_raw_sha256": "b40f91d64374285ad1e9151585d8c7b96b84a240af62b134b5998333f75286c4",
            "scope_only_not_proof_acceptance": true
          }
