    "ex-td-two-site-no-thermalization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-td-independent-factorization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let two finite microstate models from [[def-td-finite-microstate-model]] have nonempty state sets $\\Omega_A,\\Omega_B$, energies $E_A,E_B$ in joules, and the same $T>0$. Assume their declared composite model has state set $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$ and energy $E_{AB}(x,y)=E_A(x)+E_B(y)$, with no interaction term. Under the canonical sampling assumption [[post-td-canonical-sampling]], the partition function factorizes as $Z_{AB}=Z_AZ_B$, the joint canonical law is $P_{AB}=P_AP_B$, the Gibbs entropy satisfies $S_{AB}=S_A+S_B$, and the statistical free-energy value satisfies $F_{AB}=F_A+F_B$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "pthm-td-finite-mode-occupation-and-trace-formulas",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Adopt the quantum Gibbs preparation of [[post-nrqm-many-body-canonical-equilibrium-ensemble]] for a finite list of independent modes at $T>0$. For a bosonic mode with excitation energy $\\varepsilon>0$ joules, let $q=e^{-\\beta\\varepsilon}$, $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$, and let $N$ be its dimensionless occupation number. Its partition trace and mean occupation are\n$$Z_B=(1-q)^{-1},\\qquad \\langle N\\rangle_B=\\frac{q}{1-q}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}-1}.$$\nFor a fermionic two-level occupation system with energies $0,\\varepsilon$, the partition sum and excited-state occupation are\n$$Z_F=1+q,\\qquad \\langle N\\rangle_F=\\frac{q}{1+q}=\\frac1{e^{\\beta\\varepsilon}+1}.$$\nFor a finite independent collection, the corresponding partition traces multiply and each mode's marginal mean is the displayed one. These are exact model identities under the adopted preparation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as physical assumptions of the framework the following statement of isolated autonomous dynamics. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice and the Axiom of Choice ([[def-countable-choice]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]), which are the choice hypotheses of the cited spectral and group suppliers.\n\n**(i) Energy operator.** An **isolated autonomous system** of the sector has a declared **energy operator** $H_{\\mathrm{op}}:D(H_{\\mathrm{op}})\\subset H\\to H$ that is self-adjoint ([[def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator]], [[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]]) and a declared energy unit in which its spectral coordinate is read. The energy operator is part of the model specification of the system; for a massive particle it is fixed by the kinematics postulate P5, and singular or unbounded model Hamiltonians require their own self-adjointness statements.\n\n**(ii) Evolution.** The time evolution of the model is the strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group\n$$U(t):=\\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}tH_{\\mathrm{op}}\\Bigr)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R),\\qquad U(t+s)=U(t)U(s),\\quad U(t)^{*}=U(t)^{-1},$$\ndefined by the spectral calculus of the self-adjoint generator ([[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]], [[def-unbounded-integral-against-a-pvm]], [[lem-self-adjoint-operator-generates-a-strongly-continuous-unitary-group]], [[def-strongly-continuous-one-parameter-unitary-group]]). The energy unit enters the argument of the exponential so that $H_{\\mathrm{op}}t/\\hbar$ acts in the dimensionless spectral variable.\n\n**(iii) State evolution.** A vector state evolves continuously, $\\psi(t):=U(t)\\psi(0)$ for every $\\psi(0)\\in H$, and the Schroedinger equation\n$$i\\hbar\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\psi(t)=H_{\\mathrm{op}}\\psi(t)$$\nholds in the norm-differentiable sense exactly for initial vectors $\\psi(0)\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{op}})$, where the derivative is the norm limit of the difference quotients; for other vectors the equation is not asserted, although the evolution remains continuous. A density-operator preparation evolves as $\\rho(t)=U(t)\\rho(0)U(t)^{*}$, which is a density operator for every $t$ and satisfies $\\operatorname{tr}\\rho(t)=1$ ([[lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability]]).\n\n**(iv) Time-dependent families.** A prescribed time-dependent family $t\\mapsto H(t)$ of self-adjoint operators is a different model assumption: a norm-continuous finite-dimensional Hermitian family is used only through the unitary two-time propagator that its family defines, and the exponential of the integral of $H(t)$ is not identified with that propagator unless the family commutes with itself at different times. The general unbounded time-dependent case requires a separate existence and domain theorem of its own and is not adopted by this postulate; the driven-dynamics page states its own assumptions for the models it treats.\n\n**(v) Open systems.** A non-unitary evolution of the reduced description of a system coupled to an environment is an extension obtained from an explicitly chosen environment model and a declared reduction, not a replacement for clause (ii); no environment model is adopted by this postulate.\n\n**(vi) Status.** Clauses (i)-(v) are adopted assumptions of the isolated-system idealization. They assert nothing about the origin of the energy operator of a concrete system, about the accuracy of the idealization for a laboratory preparation, or about which laboratory interactions realize an isolated evolution; those are tested identifications in the sense of P7.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-framework-evolution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Assigned energy observable.** Adopt the autonomous-dynamics postulate [[post-nrqm-framework-autonomous-dynamics]] (P4) for a declared isolated system whose state space is the model's [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]]. An **assigned energy observable** of the system is an assigned sharp observable $(H_{\\mathrm{op}},u_H)$ in the sense of [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] clause (a), whose declared unit $u_H$ is an energy unit in the sense of [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]] (joules in the SI conventions fixed there). Thus $H_{\\mathrm{op}}:D(H_{\\mathrm{op}})\\subset H\\to H$ is densely defined and self-adjoint ([[def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint]], [[def-hilbert-space]]), with spectral projection-valued measure $E_H$ on the Borel sets of $\\mathbb R$ under the choice hypotheses of [[thm-spectral-theorem-for-unbounded-self-adjoint-operators]] ([[def-projection-valued-measure]]), and its outcome law in a preparation is the law of [[def-nrqm-observables-framework-observable-and-outcome-law]] clause (b). The assignment of $H_{\\mathrm{op}}$ is part of the model specification, as in clause (i) of P4; this definition asserts nothing about which laboratory quantity of a concrete system realizes it.\n\n**(b) The unitary flow.** The **time evolution** or **flow** of the isolated system is the family\n$$U(t):=\\exp\\!\\Bigl(-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}tH_{\\mathrm{op}}\\Bigr):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{-it\\lambda/\\hbar}\\,dE_H(\\lambda)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb R),$$\nthe reading of clause (ii) of P4 in the spectral calculus of $H_{\\mathrm{op}}$ ([[def-unbounded-integral-against-a-pvm]]); here $\\hbar>0$ is the reduced Planck constant with its value and unit fixed by [[def-nrqm-framework-quantities-and-units]], so that $tH_{\\mathrm{op}}/\\hbar$ is dimensionless. Equivalently, $U(t)=e^{itT}$ for the self-adjoint generator $T:=-H_{\\mathrm{op}}/\\hbar$ with $D(T)=D(H_{\\mathrm{op}})$; the two readings agree by the uniqueness of the generator in Stone's theorem, and by [[lem-self-adjoint-operator-generates-a-strongly-continuous-unitary-group]] ([[lem-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-flow-calculus]] clause (a)) the family $U$ is a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group: $U(0)=I$, $U(t+s)=U(t)U(s)$, $U(t)^{*}=U(t)^{-1}=U(-t)$ ([[def-strongly-continuous-one-parameter-unitary-group]]).\n\n**(c) Schroedinger picture.** The **Schroedinger-picture evolution** of a preparation is fixed by clause (iii) of P4. A state vector evolves by $\\psi(t):=U(t)\\psi(0)$ for every $\\psi(0)\\in H$, and the **state at time $t$** of the pure preparation $[\\psi(0)]$ is the pure preparation $[\\psi(t)]$, which is well defined because $U(t)$ is injective; a general preparation $\\rho$, that is an element of the state space $D(H)$ of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-framework-state-space]], evolves by\n$$\\rho(t):=U(t)\\rho(0)U(t)^{*},$$\nthe reading of clause (iii) of P4 in the density-operator vocabulary of [[def-nrqm-state-geometry-pure-mixed-states-and-purity]] ([[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]], [[lem-nrqm-composites-density-and-scalar-spectral-probability]]). In this picture the assigned observables are held fixed at their assigned values and the preparation moves.\n\n**(d) Heisenberg picture.** For a **bounded** assigned sharp observable $(A,u_A)$ — that is, an assigned sharp observable with $A\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]), so that $A$ is self-adjoint and everywhere defined — the **Heisenberg representative** of $(A,u_A)$ at time $t$ is the pair $(A_H(t),u_A)$ with the same declared unit and\n$$A_H(t):=U(t)^{*}AU(t)=U(-t)AU(t).$$\nBy [[lem-nrqm-autonomous-dynamics-heisenberg-flow]] clauses (a) and (d), $A_H(t)$ is again bounded and self-adjoint with spectral projection-valued measure $E_{A_H(t)}(B)=U(-t)E_A(B)U(t)$, so $(A_H(t),u_A)$ is again an assigned sharp observable of the framework; in this picture the preparation is held fixed at its value at time $0$ and the observables move. The two pictures are two bookkeeping devices for the same model; the statement that they assign the same outcome laws is proved in the Heisenberg-picture theorem of this page, not assumed here.\n\n**(e) Stationary preparations.** A preparation $\\rho\\in D(H)$ is **stationary** for the assigned energy observable when $\\rho(t)=\\rho$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$. A pure preparation $[\\psi]$ is stationary when $[\\psi(t)]=[\\psi(0)]$ for every $t$, that is, when the evolved vector remains in the ray of $\\psi$; the characterization of this condition by the eigenrays of $H_{\\mathrm{op}}$ is proved in the stationary-states theorem of this page, not assumed here. An **energy eigenstate** is a pure preparation of a unit eigenvector of $H_{\\mathrm{op}}$, and a **stationary state** in the model is a stationary pure preparation; by that theorem the two notions coincide.\n\n**(f) Energy-compatible observables.** An assigned sharp observable $(A,u_A)$ is **compatible with the energy**, or **energy-compatible**, when its spectral projection-valued measure and that of the energy commute strongly,\n$$E_A(B)E_H(C)=E_H(C)E_A(B)\\qquad\\text{for all Borel }B,C\\subseteq\\mathbb R .$$\nThis is an equality of bounded operators on $H$, not a relation between formal commutators on a small domain; the phrase **conserved quantity** for an energy-compatible assigned observable is a claim about the time invariance of its outcome law and is established in the energy-conservation theorem of this page for bounded $A$.\n\n**(g) Status.** Clauses (a)-(f) record the readings of P4 together with P1 and P2 in the vocabulary of this page and fix the terminology used by the later pages of the category. They adopt no physical assumption beyond P4, P1 and P2 and the choice hypotheses of the cited mathematics, assert no dynamics for a prescribed time-dependent family or for an open system (clauses (iv) and (v) of P4 are separate model assumptions treated on their own pages), assert no measurement model, no apparatus realization and no empirical result, and make no claim that any laboratory system is exactly isolated.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1]–[F2], the finite-temperature Gibbs probabilities on $(00,01,10,11)$ are $(4/9,2/9,2/9,1/9)$. In particular the Gibbs probability of $|11\\rangle$ is $1/9>0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Since $H|00\\rangle=0$, $U_t|00\\rangle=|00\\rangle$ for every $t$; thus $\\rho(t)=|00\\rangle\\langle00|$. Its probability for the projector $|11\\rangle\\langle11|$ is zero at every time, unlike the Gibbs value $1/9$. The state therefore never reaches this Gibbs density under the declared unitary evolution.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
